Mitcad patch (mitcad#343): roundings of both edges of a thread's crest
narrower than the roundings, one blend between the two flanks cut by the
faces at the thread's ends.

A thread's crest 0.8 wide, both its edges rounded with 0.8: the two
roundings' contacts on the crest cross each other, and OverlappingStripes
computes each again overflowing the crest, the ball touching both flanks.
At each end of the thread the face at the end (a plane square to the axis,
a chamfer cone) meets the helix at about a degree, and it cuts that blend
along a curve 83 long: one flank's contact runs on past the spine's end by
17 before it meets the face, the other's meets it 65 before. Five things
kept OCCT from that blend:

- The walk past the spine's end follows the guide, prolonged along the end
  edge's own curve or its face's iso-line as far as these go on (0742),
  then towards a far point along its end tangent (ChFi3d_PerformElSpine);
  the crest's helices and the flanks' iso-lines stop about where their
  faces do. 60 past the end of a helix of radius 28 the sections turned
  more than a radian away from the ball's path, and
  ChFi3d_CutSingularExtension cut the walk back before its contacts
  reached the face at the end. A guide that a screw motion maps onto
  itself (within a ten-thousandth of its length) is now continued by that
  motion where 0742's extension does not reach far enough, a turn at most
  (ChFi3d_ScrewContinuedCurve: a copy of its end moved by the motion,
  joined with a knot of multiplicity the degree, the guide itself kept).
- The flank whose contact runs on past the spine's end is a B-spline
  surface ending about where its face ends; the walk evaluated it far past
  its bounds, on its end spans' polynomials, and lost the ball. The walks'
  surfaces (ConexFaces, StartSolOverflow) now take a B-spline surface that
  a screw motion sweeps along one of its parameters as continued by that
  motion past both ends, by a turn or its own length at most
  (ChFi3d_ScrewContinued, ChFi3d_LoadScrewContinued): its own poles and
  knots over its range, so that the contacts' p-curves are the face's.
- At the chamfer cone the flank's contact crosses onto the cone, which
  meets the flank at 0.8 degree, within the tenth of a radian SearchFace
  takes for a face continued: the next walk rolled the ball on the cone.
  A face at that end of the spine (holding its end vertex) whose normals
  along the edge are nowhere within the angular tolerance of the face left
  is now the face at the end, which cuts the blend (KinkIntoEndFace in
  ComputeData).
- The two stripes' blends are one, but each stripe ran out to the faces at
  the ends at one end only (or the second's start section lay on faces
  elsewhere): RemoveCoincidentStripes kept both and their corners
  failed. Where one of the pair is a single piece on the two faces beyond
  the crest whose contacts both end on edges at both its ends, the other is
  dropped with its corners (DropOverlapsRunOutByOther).
- Two robustness fixes on the way: a start section of a stripe computed
  again over the crest is taken only where the ball touches the face
  across the other stripe's edge and its own edge's other face
  (StartSolOverflow: near the crest's end a section ran on to the root and
  the end plane), and the test whether two contacts overlap
  (contactBeyond) takes the contact at its interference's parameters and
  projects on the other within its range (on a line of three turns a
  projection landed a turn away, and the pair went undetected).

